.TH "LMM" "1" "Nov 2026" "lmm 2.3.0" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
lmm-profile-import - Import a profile


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBlmm profile import [file] [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Import a profile from a file - an lmm collection (.lmmc, or its YAML
document) or an older profile export - or from a Steam Workshop collection.

.PP
Missing mods are downloaded and installed automatically, after a
confirmation prompt; pass -y/--yes to skip the prompt and answer yes. Use
--no-install to skip that entirely and just save the profile, installing
nothing. Use --force to overwrite an existing profile with the same name
instead of failing.

.PP
An lmm collection (what 'lmm profile export' writes) is matched to one of
your games by its Steam app id, then by its source ids; when none or
several match, choose with --game. The plan shows the collection's
description and instructions, its hooks, its config overrides, the items
lmm cannot fetch (to get by hand), and mods from sources you do not have.
A collection that needs features this version of lmm does not have is
refused, with nothing written. --as names the imported profile.

.PP
Whatever the file's format, two things are held back until you ask, and
both are listed before anything is written. Hooks are scripts lmm would
run, so they are imported only with --with-hooks. Config overrides are
imported when they are text config files (.ini, .cfg, .conf, .txt, .json,
\&.xml, .toml, .yaml, .yml, .properties, .config, .ltx, .lsx, .cvars,
\&.ron), except loader configurations that can make the game load a DLL
(doorstop_config.ini, enblocal.ini); those and any other file type are
skipped unless you pass --allow-any-overrides. The result says what was
held back.

.PP
--workshop-collection takes a Steam Workshop collection id or the URL of
its page, and imports the collection AS a profile: a collection is a mod
list, which is what a profile is. It needs no API key. The profile RECORDS
the list: items you are already subscribed to (and have tracked with 'lmm
import --workshop') are marked as such, and the rest are listed with what
to do about them - lmm cannot download a Workshop item you are not
subscribed to. The profile is named after the collection unless you name
it with --as.

.PP
A Workshop item is game-global: Steam has it on disk and the game loads it
whichever profile is active. So switching to a collection profile deploys
nothing, and the profile is a record of what the collection contains
rather than a set you turn on and off.

.PP
Examples:
  lmm profile import survival.yaml --game skyrim-se
  lmm profile import survival.yaml --game skyrim-se --yes
  lmm profile import survival.yaml --game skyrim-se --no-install
  lmm profile import survival.yaml --game skyrim-se --force
  lmm profile import survival.lmmc
  lmm profile import survival.lmmc --game skyrim-se --with-hooks --as survival-2
  lmm profile import --workshop-collection 2500900001 --game space-engineers-2
  lmm profile import --workshop-collection https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=2500900001 --as ships


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--allow-any-overrides\fP[=false]
	import overrides that are not text config files too (listed in the plan; skipped without this)

.PP
\fB--as\fP=""
	name the imported profile (a collection, --workshop-collection or --nexus-collection; an older profile export names its own)

.PP
\fB--fomod-choices\fP=""
	answer FOMOD installer questions from a JSON file (the profile's fomod: shape or Vortex's choices array; key it by source:id when several mods ask, or by source:id#file-id for one file's installer)

.PP
\fB--fomod-defaults\fP[=false]
	answer FOMOD installer questions with the installer's own preselection

.PP
\fB--force\fP[=false]
	overwrite existing profile

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for import

.PP
\fB--nexus-collection\fP=""
	import a NexusMods collection (its slug, slug@revision, or the URL of its page) instead of a file

.PP
\fB--no-install\fP[=false]
	skip installing missing mods

.PP
\fB--with-hooks\fP[=false]
	import the hooks the file carries (they are scripts lmm runs; listed in the plan)

.PP
\fB--with-optional\fP[=false]
	with --nexus-collection, install the collection's optional mods too (they are left out by default)

.PP
\fB--workshop-collection\fP=""
	import a Steam Workshop collection (its id, or the URL of its page) instead of a file

.PP
\fB-y\fP, \fB--yes\fP[=false]
	auto-confirm downloading/installing missing mods


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--config\fP=""
	config directory (default: $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lmm or ~/.config/lmm)

.PP
\fB--data\fP=""
	data directory (default: $XDG_DATA_HOME/lmm or ~/.local/share/lmm)

.PP
\fB-g\fP, \fB--game\fP=""
	game ID to operate on

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	output JSON instead of text; mutating commands print their result, --dry-run prints the plan; never prompts

.PP
\fB--log-level\fP="off"
	diagnostic log level written to stderr (off, error, warn, info, debug)

.PP
\fB--no-color\fP[=false]
	disable colored output (NO_COLOR env is also honored)

.PP
\fB--no-hooks\fP[=false]
	disable all hooks

.PP
\fB-v\fP, \fB--verbose\fP[=false]
	verbose output


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBlmm-profile(1)\fP


